"""The TV twin: a fictional television set (the Vireo 55 receiver and its VR-7 remote control) written as a specification exported
from a requirements-management tool. Same layout as the greenhouse twin (../build_twin_spec_fixture.py), with a logo in the running header
and a tilted CONFIDENTIAL stamp beside the cover title. It is the one source for the TV twin in every format: build_tv_twin_docx.py renders
the same SECTIONS as a Word document, and build_tv_gold.py derives the target tree (tv_gold_tree.json) that every format must give.

- a deep running header grid (label and value rows) on every page, whose lower rows sit inside the body area, with a company logo;
- a three-line notice rotated 90 degrees in the left margin of every page, and a tilted stamp on the cover;
- headings at body size found only through the bookmark outline;
- lowercase requirement codes with no separator (``abc_12``) in a hanging-indent column beside the requirement text;
- under each requirement, a trace label and its parent codes one per line, and Notes inside the requirement column or full width;
- lists, coded sub-items (``men_3.a``), one uncoded requirement, requirements and lists that cross a page, sections whose whole body is
  "None." or "Not applicable.", requirements with no obligation word, and a traceability listing of every code as back matter.

``build_twin_spec_pdf(out_dir)`` writes tv_twin_spec.pdf and returns ``(path, truth)``; ``truth`` is the tree the document states.
Every name, value and sentence is invented."""

